<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as PEPSU</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Patiala and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-punjab">East Punjab</a> States Union was a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> formed in 1948 by merging eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, including Patiala, Nabha, Jind, Kapurthala, Faridkot, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malerkotla">Malerkotla</a> and Kalsia, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Patiala as its head. Its capital was Patiala. In 1956 it was merged into the state of Punjab.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union.jpg" alt="Patiala and East Punjab States Union" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Superbenjamin this file: Furfur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PEPSU_in_India_(1951).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>PEPSU was inaugurated on 15 July 1948 from six salute states, Patiala, Jind, Kapurthala, Nabha, Faridkot and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malerkotla">Malerkotla</a>, and two non-salute states, Kalsia and Nalagarh, which kept their native rulers; it formally became a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1950. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Patiala, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadavindra-singh">Yadavindra Singh</a>, was its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a>, equivalent to a governor, for its whole existence, and the Maharaja of Kapurthala, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagatjit-singh">Jagatjit Singh</a>, served as Uparajpramukh. Gian Singh Rarewala was sworn in as the first Chief Minister on 13 January 1949, and Col. Raghbir Singh succeeded him on 23 May 1951.</p><p>The state elected a 60-member legislative assembly on 6 January 1952, in which the Congress Party won 26 seats and the Akali Dal 19. On 22 April 1952 Rarewala became Chief Minister again at the head of the United Front, a coalition of the Akali Dal and independents, but his government was dismissed on 5 March 1953 and President's rule imposed. After the mid-term poll the Congress won a majority and Raghbir Singh returned on 8 March 1954; on his death Brish Bhan became Chief Minister on 12 January 1955, the last to hold the office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>PEPSU covered 26,208 km2 and in 1951 had a population of 3,493,685, of whom 19% lived in towns, spread over 64 towns and 5,708 villages. It began in 1948 with eight districts and 25 tehsils. In 1953 the districts were reduced to five: Fatehgarh Sahib and Kohistan were merged with Patiala, and Barnala was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangrur">Sangrur</a> and Bhatinda. The state returned members to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lok-sabha">Lok Sabha</a> from four constituencies, one of them, Kapurthala-Bhatinda, a double-seat constituency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Under the States Reorganisation Act, PEPSU was merged mostly into Punjab State on 1 November 1956. Parts of it, including the present Jind district, Pinjore, Charkhi Dadri, Bawal and Mahendragarh Narnaul, now lie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>, which was separated from Punjab on 1 November 1966, and others, among them Solan and Nalagarh, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Patiala and East Punjab States Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
